the motion for a flat sheet vs crumpled sheet. Write a gravity definition in your words. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about gravity linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Thinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready).. Correction: False — acts on all objects; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready). Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses gravity. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about gravity, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about gravity. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for gravity. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for gravity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","False — acts on all objects","Thinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready)","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"True or fix: “Gravity only works on heavy things.” “Gravity pulls down toward Earth.” Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_inde