ch the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Physical World","success_criteria":["I can use gravity with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify fall and explain why it matters.","I can practise pull independently on a new prompt.","I can notice Earth needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Gravity is a pull toward Earth that makes unsupported objects fall and gives us weight.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Gravity is a pull toward Earth that makes unsupported objects fall and gives us weight","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about gravity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDrop a ball and a scarf: both fall, though air can slow some objects. Jump up — gravity pulls you back down. Scales measure weight from gravity’s pull.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTrue or fix: “Gravity only works on heavy things.” “Gravity pulls down toward Earth.”\n\nFEEDBACK\nFalse — acts on all objects; second statement is the useful Year 3 idea.","explain":"Gravity is a pull toward Earth that makes unsupported objects fall and gives us weight. On Earth it acts downward toward the ground.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready). Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Gravity idea.","caption":"Acts on all objects.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Gravity</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"