 observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Everyday gravity evidence.","caption":"Write your definition.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Drop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">ball and scarf</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Both</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fall downward</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about gravity with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready). Here is a more productive model: Drop a ball and a scarf: both fall, though air can slow some objects. Jump up — gravity pulls you back down. Scales measure weight from gravity’s pull.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFalse — acts on all objects; second statement is the useful Year 3 idea.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking astronauts have “no gravity” in orbit oversimplifies — they are in free fall (stretch idea only if ready). Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Use t